 Small operations repository for the log retention job.
 
-`bin/archive-logs.sh SERVICE` packs the logs of one service from `logs/` into a dated
-tarball under `archives/`. It runs from cron on the log host every night.
+`bin/archive-logs.sh [--keep-days N] SERVICE [SERVICE...]` packs the logs of each named
+service from `logs/` into a dated tarball (`archives/SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz`). It runs from
+cron on the log host every night, from the repository directory.
 
-Archives are pruned by hand today.
+```
+bin/archive-logs.sh web api
+bin/archive-logs.sh --keep-days 30 web api
+```
+
+- Service names must match `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$`. All names are checked before anything
+  is archived, and repeated names are archived once.
+- `--keep-days N` (N a positive integer) removes archives older than N days, by modification
+  time, after the new archives are in place. It only touches files named
+  `SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz` directly in `archives/`, for every service, not just those named
+  on the command line. If any service fails to archive, nothing is pruned.
+- On success it prints one summary line, for example
+  `archived 2 service(s): web api; pruned 3 archive(s) older than 30 day(s)`
+  (the `pruned` part appears only with `--keep-days`).
+- Exit status: 0 on success, 1 if a service could not be archived, 64 for a usage error,
+  65 for an invalid service name or `--keep-days` value.
+
+Requires Bash 4.4 or later and GNU `find` and `tar`. Archives are created with umask 027.
